      <div class="msv__content">
        <p>The TIE card (Tarjeta de Identidad de Extranjero) is one of the most important documents you&#x27;ll encounter as a foreigner in Spain. It&#x27;s your official identity card as a foreign resident, required for virtually every interaction with Spanish authorities. This comprehensive guide explains what the TIE card is, who needs it, and how to obtain one.</p>
<h2>What Is the TIE Card?</h2>
<p>The TIE card is Spain&#x27;s official identity document for foreign nationals. It&#x27;s a physical card that proves your legal residency status in Spain and serves as your primary identification. Unlike a passport, the TIE card is specifically recognized within Spain as proof of legal residence. It functions as a combination of an ID card, proof of residency, and immigration document.</p>
<p>Official Name and Purpose</p>
<p>TIE stands for Tarjeta de Identidad de Extranjero (Foreign Identity Card). It&#x27;s issued by Spain&#x27;s immigration authorities (the extranjería) and serves as the definitive proof that you have legal residency status in Spain. Both Spanish citizens and foreign residents interact with the TIE card regularly in administrative, banking, and legal contexts.</p>
<p>Physical Appearance</p>
<p>The TIE card is a plastic card similar in size to a credit card. It displays your photograph, full name, nationality, date of birth, NIE number (your foreign resident identification number), residency status (temporary or permanent), and validity dates. For temporary residents, the expiration date matches your residency permit. Permanent residents&#x27; cards have no expiration date.</p>
<h2>Who Needs a TIE Card?</h2>
<p>Any non-Spanish national with legal residency in Spain must obtain a TIE card. This includes workers, entrepreneurs, students, non-lucrative residents, and digital nomads. The only exceptions are EU/EEA citizens and their family members, who have different documentation requirements under EU free movement rules.</p>
<h2>The NIE Number: Your TIE Foundation</h2>
<p>Before obtaining a TIE card, you must have a NIE number (Número de Identidad de Extranjero—Foreigner Identification Number). The NIE is a unique nine-character identification code assigned to every foreign resident. It serves as your tax identification number and is used in all Spanish administrative systems.</p>
<p>When You Get Your NIE</p>
<p>You typically receive a NIE number before arriving in Spain. Your visa application process usually generates a NIE. Once your residency visa is approved, you receive a NIE letter from the Spanish consulate. You must bring this letter when you arrive in Spain and during your TIE application process.</p>
<p>What Your NIE Looks Like</p>
<p>The NIE is formatted as a letter followed by 7 digits and another letter (e.g., X1234567Y). The initial letter typically corresponds to your visa category. You&#x27;ll see your NIE on your visa stamp, your TIE card, tax documents, and bank statements.</p>
<h2>How to Obtain Your TIE Card: Step-by-Step</h2>
<p>Step 1: Register at Local Police</p>
<p>Within the first 30 days of arriving in Spain, you must register with the Policía Nacional (National Police). Visit your local police station and register your presence in Spain. You&#x27;ll receive a registration confirmation. This step is essential—you cannot proceed with TIE applications without this registration.</p>
<p>Step 2: Register at the Extranjería</p>
<p>Report to your provincial immigration office (extranjería) to formally register as a resident. You&#x27;ll submit your visa, passport, and other documentation. The extranjería will verify your legal entry and residency status. This registration is distinct from police registration and is essential for TIE issuance.</p>
<p>Step 3: Schedule Your TIE Appointment</p>
<p>Once you&#x27;ve registered at the extranjería, you can schedule your TIE appointment. Most offices offer online appointment booking through their websites. You&#x27;ll need to select a date within 30–60 days. Many provinces are heavily backlogged, so appointments may be difficult to secure.</p>
<p>Step 4: Attend Your TIE Appointment</p>
<p>Bring your passport, visa entry stamp, police registration confirmation, extranjería registration receipt, completed TIE application (model EX-17), two recent passport-style photographs, proof of accommodation, and any documents required by your specific province. Arrive early; processing typically takes 20–30 minutes.</p>
<p>Step 5: Pay the TIE Fee</p>
<p>You&#x27;ll pay a fee for your TIE card (typically €10–€15). Payment is accepted at the extranjería or through a separate payment process. Keep your receipt—it proves you&#x27;ve paid and is necessary for collection.</p>
<p>Step 6: Collect Your TIE Card</p>
<p>Your TIE card is issued within 7–15 days of your appointment. You&#x27;ll receive a notification (typically via email or postal mail) when it&#x27;s ready. Return to the extranjería to collect your physical card. You must present your receipt, complete the collection form, and verify the information on your card is correct.</p>
<p>Need help navigating the TIE application?</p>
<p>Our specialists can assist with appointments, documentation, and ensure your TIE is processed without delays.</p>
