<h2>Common Delays and How to Avoid Them</h2>
<p>Don&#x27;t miss your appointment—rescheduling is difficult. Don&#x27;t bring incomplete documentation; verify requirements at your local extranjería before attending. Don&#x27;t apply for TIE before registering with police and extranjería; these steps are mandatory prerequisites. Don&#x27;t expect collection to be instant; TIE cards take 1–2 weeks after appointment.</p>
<h2>TIE Card Renewal</h2>
<p>Your TIE card is valid for the duration of your residency permit. For temporary residents, renewal is required when your residency permit expires. You&#x27;ll follow a similar process: schedule an appointment, attend in person, pay fees, and collect your new card. Permanent residents&#x27; TIE cards never expire and don&#x27;t require renewal.</p>
<h2>Using Your TIE Card in Daily Life</h2>
<p>Your TIE card is your primary identification in Spain. You&#x27;ll present it when: opening a bank account, signing rental agreements, registering utilities, accessing healthcare, enrolling in educational institutions, traveling outside Spain, and conducting any administrative or legal transaction. Carrying your TIE card is recommended, though most Spaniards carry their national ID cards instead.</p>
